17592186158093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158092 #edb/ref 1061 231907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158092 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c8b4e298-ed67-50ea-bb52-8968e4cd58bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158092 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158093 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158093 #edb/ref 1061 231908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158093 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "91d86e1a-5d29-5149-9978-86770afc6de0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158093 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158094 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158094 #edb/ref 1061 231911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158094 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "deee50a7-b19c-5fb9-80a5-a024ec3943c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158094 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158095 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158095 #edb/ref 1061 231912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158095 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c9bdde30-6bf2-5ffc-93a3-92c41bf6792d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158095 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158096 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158096 #edb/ref 1061 231915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158096 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f4444ff2-15d1-524f-952e-a3223b79ead3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158096 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158097 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158097 #edb/ref 1061 231918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158097 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "82e8fc5c-326f-538e-acdc-fbdce35a6bb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158097 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158098 #edb/ref 1061 231919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158098 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62349611-970a-5bd8-b8c7-3d773fb63239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158098 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158424 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158424 #edb/ref 1061 610975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158424 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b87874f9-2306-59b4-a907-b5f36d18a293" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158424 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158432 #edb/ref 1064 "We can extend a scalar function $f(x)$ to a square matrix argument $A.$ This function $f(A)$ is called a **matrix function**, and maps a square matrix to another square matrix of the same size.\n\nGiven a square matrix $A$ and a scalar function $f$ with Maclaurin series\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty c_k x^k,\n$$\n\nwe can define $f(A)$ by replacing $x$ with $A,$ with addition and multiplication performed in the matrix sense:\n\n$$\nf(A) = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty c_k A^k\n$$\n\nFor example, recall that the Maclaurin series of $f(x) = e^x$ is\n\n$$\ne^x = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\dfrac{x^k}{k!}.\n$$\n\nThen, for a square matrix $A,$ we define the matrix exponential as\n\n$$\ne^A = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{k!}A^k.\n$$\n\nIn the next slide, we'll see how to apply matrix functions to square matrices." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158432 #edb/ref 1065 10688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158432 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c3451824-f52b-539c-b16f-3819d3ea37df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158432 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158433 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose $D$ is a diagonal matrix with diagonal entries $\\lambda_1, \\ldots, \\lambda_n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {\\lambda }_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor a scalar function $f$ with a Maclaurin series expansion, $f(D)$ is the diagonal matrix with diagonal entries $f(\\lambda_1), \\ldots, f(\\lambda_n){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & f({\\lambda }_{2}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & f({\\lambda }_{n})\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor example, given the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the function $f(x) = e^x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(2) & 0 \\\\ 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis rule works because raising a diagonal matrix to a power is equivalent to raising each diagonal entry to the same power.\n\nIndeed, if\n$\\displaystyle\nf(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty c_k x^k$\nis the Maclaurin series of $f,$ then, by the definition of $f(D),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(D) & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{D}^{k} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {\\lambda }_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}^{k} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1}^{k} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}^{k} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {\\lambda }_{n}^{k}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{\\lambda }_{1}^{k} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{\\lambda }_{2}^{k} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{\\lambda }_{n}^{k}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}f({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & f({\\lambda }_{2}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & f({\\lambda }_{n})\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186158433 #edb/ref 1065 10689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158433 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "da162714-f641-5ded-aec8-6f24c0d5cbb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158433 #edb/ref 1067 "Function of a Diagonal Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158434 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose $J_n(\\lambda)$ is an $n\\times n$ Jordan block where the main diagonal entries are all $\\lambda{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\lambda  & 1 & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda  & 1 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & \\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & \\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor a scalar function $f$ analytic at $\\lambda$ (i.e., has a Taylor series expansion at $\\lambda$), $f(J_n(\\lambda))$ is the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{'}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{''}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{'}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{'}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $f(J_3(2))$ for $f(x) = \\ln x.$\n\nSince $J_3(2)$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, we will need the first $3-1=2$ derivatives of $f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}x \\\\ {f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{''}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/43/439d1a1c3c2c315510fd293e8bb8e1467e149222025359a0e0d899270eb07e12.svg)\n\nNow that we can compute the value of a function of a Jordan block, we can also find the value of a function of a Jordan matrix.\n\nThe matrix [math]f(J_n(\\lambda))[/math] for [math]n=2,3,[/math] and [math]4[/math] are as follows.\n\n* For [math]2\\times 2[/math] Jordan blocks, we have\n\\[\nf(J_2(\\lambda)) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\tf(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & f(\\lambda)\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\n* For [math]3\\times 3[/math] Jordan blocks, we have\n\\[\nf(J_3(\\lambda)) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\tf(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) & \\dfrac12f''(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & f(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & 0 & f(\\lambda)\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\n* For [math]4\\times 4[/math] Jordan blocks, we have\n\\[\nf(J_4(\\lambda)) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\tf(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) & \\dfrac12f''(\\lambda) & \\dfrac16f'''(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & f(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) & \\dfrac12f''(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & 0 & f(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & 0 & 0 & f(\\lambda)\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186158434 #edb/ref 1065 10691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158434 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7972371e-9205-5e74-b277-6dfcab332f9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158434 #edb/ref 1067 "Function of a Jordan Block" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158435 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose $A$ and $S$ are matrices, where $S$ is invertible.\nFor a scalar function $f$ with a Maclaurin series expansion, we have\n\n$$\nf(SAS^{-1}) = Sf(A)S^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIndeed, given the Maclaurin series $\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty c_kx^k,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(SA{S}^{-1}) & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}(SA{S}^{-1})^{k} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}S{A}^{k}{S}^{-1} \\\\  & =S({\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{A}^{k}){S}^{-1} \\\\  & =Sf(A){S}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo far, we have seen how to evaluate functions for diagonal matrices and Jordan blocks. With the formula above, we will now be able to\n\n- evaluate $f(B)$ for diagonalizable $B$ using a diagonalization $B=PDP^{-1},$ and\n\n- evaluate $f(B)$ for non-diagonalizable $B$ using a Jordan decomposition $B=PJP^{-1}.$\n\nLet's see how with an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158435 #edb/ref 1065 10692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158435 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4241af18-972e-530c-b06b-388437001b24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158435 #edb/ref 1067 "Function of a Jordan Decomposition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158466 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a Jordan matrix formed by the direct sum of blocks $J_{n_1}(\\lambda_1) {\\textstyle\\:\\oplus\\:} \\cdots {\\textstyle\\:\\oplus\\:} J_{n_k}(\\lambda_k)$ and a function $f.$ By applying the rule for diagonal matrices to this block diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{{n}_{1}}({\\lambda }_{1})\\,⊕\\,⋯\\,⊕\\,{J}_{{n}_{k}}({\\lambda }_{k})) & =f(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{{n}_{1}}({\\lambda }_{1}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & {J}_{{n}_{k}}({\\lambda }_{k})\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}f({J}_{{n}_{1}}({\\lambda }_{1})) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & f({J}_{{n}_{k}}({\\lambda }_{k}))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =f({J}_{{n}_{1}}({\\lambda }_{1}))\\,⊕\\,⋯\\,⊕\\,f({J}_{{n}_{k}}({\\lambda }_{k})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis allows us to compute functions of any matrix in Jordan Normal Form by applying the function to each Jordan block individually.\n\nFor example, suppose $J = J_2(2) \\oplus J_1(3).$ Then, applying a function $f,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f({J}_{2}(2)) & 0 \\\\ 0 & f({J}_{1}(3))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186158466 #edb/ref 1065 14947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158466 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e85abcea-5a44-50b9-9104-b71309622287" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158466 #edb/ref 1067 "Function of a Jordan Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290713 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186290713 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290713 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "137e534f-bd6f-5c02-a6f5-0e5fdadf5e06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290734 #edb/ref 1080 "3\\operatorname{ln}3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290734 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290734 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1895e02e-cbf5-58b0-9b56-73c0b66223b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290849 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290849 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290849 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "357699af-2b79-5d63-9e6a-60e4e15f1367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290921 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186290921 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290921 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "462c0bc3-ae5d-5e7d-ba6c-584073583cc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290987 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290987 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17