592186292563 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | $3$ | {{field-1}} | $\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${3}^{({J}_{3}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2))}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $3$ | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $3$ | $0$ |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-4}} |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292563 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f9fe47a-1d49-50f6-9c5d-ebf149ad2b0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292563 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2({J}_{3}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2))} & ={3}^{(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{3}(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{1}(2)\\end{bmatrix})} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{({J}_{3}(1))} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {3}^{({J}_{1}(2))}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={3}^{({J}_{3}(1))}\\,⊕\\,{3}^{({J}_{1}(2))}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(1),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ Since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivative of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={3}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(1)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{1} & {3}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {3}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2} \\\\ 0 & {3}^{1} & {3}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {3}^{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & \\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(2),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =1.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(2)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{(2)}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{({J}_{3}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2))} & ={3}^{({J}_{3}(1))}\\,⊕\\,{3}^{({J}_{1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & \\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186363587 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363587 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363587 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363587 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186363588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363587 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0806ead2-441e-535f-8c5f-faa5d813ac46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363588 #edb/ref 1080 "125" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363588 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363588 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "afd8d41a-bb06-5cea-8b85-8050627b3550" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363589 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363589 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363589 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363589 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186363590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363589 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d3ae8ac8-e420-5629-92cc-c96a0a86fefb" #edb/ref 17592186